  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nCurrent water vapor imagery and RAP analysis indicate continued \nnorthwest flow aloft over the Plains as the midlevel ridge builds \nover the Desert Southwest to the east. At the surface, high pressure \nresides across the Middle Missouri Valley and Great Lakes while lee \ntroughing commences across the High Plains. \n\nA mid/upper impulse racing across the Canadian Prairie toward the \nGreat Lakes later tonight into Tuesday will drive a surface trough \nthrough the Northern Plains. A tightening pressure gradient ahead of \nthis feature will promote somewhat breezy south winds throughout the \nday, which will aid in the return of seasonably warm temperatures in \nthe low to middle 80s across the forecast area. The \naforementioned surface trough will look to stall across the \nforecast area Wednesday into Thursday which may allow for \nslightly cooler temperatures across central KS. However, for the\nmost part there appears to be little change in the seasonably \nwarm and dry pattern through the upcoming weekend with ridging \nremaining in place.\n\nA potential change may arrive early next week with the long-range \nsuite highlighting a potent shortwave digging into the Four Corners \nand ejecting into the Plains. Though confidence in this scenario is \nlow at this time, this feature may bring moisture relief and cooler \ntemperatures to the area toward the latter half of next week. Stay \ntuned as additional information becomes available.",
